    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sanskrit, the sacred and learned language of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is first known from the hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, composed around 1500–1200 BCE and handed down by memory with remarkable precision ever since.</li><li>Codified by the grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> around the 4th century BCE, Classical Sanskrit became the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scripture, the great epics, philosophy, science and poetry, and of Buddhist and Jain learning, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>.</li><li>Its study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the late 18th century revealed the Indo-European family of languages, and it remains the liturgical language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and a source of words for the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1786 the British judge William Jones told the Asiatic Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> that Sanskrit, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> were so alike that they must have sprung from a common source, “which, perhaps, no longer exists”: a founding moment of comparative linguistics.</p></aside>
    </section>

