      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1500–1200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> are composed in the north-west of the subcontinent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1380 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A treaty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> invokes the Indo-Aryan gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, Varuna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> composes the Ashtadhyayi, his grammar of Sanskrit in some four thousand rules</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 150</span><span class="rf-text">The Junagadh inscription of Rudradaman, the first long royal inscription in Sanskrit</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">499</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a> writes his treatise on astronomy and mathematics in Sanskrit verse</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1786</span><span class="rf-text">William Jones points out the common origin of Sanskrit, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s constitution lists Sanskrit among the languages of the nation</span></li></ol>
