    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rigveda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rigveda.jpg" alt="Rigveda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rigveda_MS2097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanskrit belongs to the Indo-Aryan branch of the Indo-European family, and with Avestan, the language of the Zoroastrian scriptures, it forms the Indo-Iranian group. Most linguists and historians hold that speakers of Indo-Aryan languages entered the north-west of the subcontinent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 2nd millennium BCE, though the question is debated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Indo-Aryan words also appear far to the west: a treaty of about 1380 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> invokes the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, Varuna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, and a Mitanni manual on horse training uses Indo-Aryan numbers.</p><p>The oldest form of the language, Vedic Sanskrit, is preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of the Brahmanical tradition. The oldest of them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, a collection of 1,028 hymns to the gods in ten books, was composed in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> around 1500 to 1200 BCE. For many centuries it was not written down at all: priests learned the hymns by heart and recited them using elaborate techniques, such as repeating the words in different orders, which preserved the text with extraordinary accuracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognised this tradition of Vedic chanting as part of the intangible heritage of humanity in 2003.</p><p>The later Vedic texts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, which explain the rituals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, which explore the nature of the self and ultimate reality, show the language changing towards its classical form.</p><p>The name saṃskṛta means “refined” or “perfected”, in contrast to prākṛta, “natural”, the name given to the everyday languages that developed alongside it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrits</a>. Sanskrit was thus defined from early times as the polished, correct form of speech, used for ritual, learning and literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/devanagari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devanagari.jpg" alt="Devanagari" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Devanagari_Varnamala.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Symphonicallyu, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanskrit has a rich system of sounds, which Indian grammarians described with great precision. It distinguishes consonants by whether they are voiced and whether they are aspirated, so that k, kh, g and gh are four distinct sounds, and it has a series of retroflex consonants, pronounced with the tongue curled back, which it shares with other languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Vedic Sanskrit had a pitch accent, which disappeared in the classical language. When words meet in a sentence, their sounds change according to rules called sandhi, and these changes are written, which can join a whole phrase into a single string of letters.</p><p>Its grammar is highly inflected. Nouns have eight cases, three numbers, singular, dual and plural, and three genders; verbs have a great variety of forms for tense, mood and voice. Sanskrit also forms compounds of great length, especially in poetry and learned prose, where a single word can carry the meaning of a whole sentence.</p><p>The grammar of Sanskrit was codified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, who lived in the north-west, probably in the 4th century BCE. His Ashtadhyayi, “Eight Chapters”, describes the language in some four thousand concise rules, using technical symbols and principles for applying rules that anticipate modern formal grammars. Later grammarians, Katyayana and Patanjali, commented on it, and Panini’s work fixed Classical Sanskrit in a form that changed little thereafter. It is often regarded as one of the greatest intellectual achievements of antiquity.</p><p>Sanskrit has no script of its own. The earliest Indian inscriptions, the edicts of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> in the 3rd century BCE, are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrits</a> written in the Brahmi and Kharosthi scripts, and Sanskrit inscriptions appear from about the 1st century BCE. Over the centuries Sanskrit was written in many scripts derived from Brahmi, such as Grantha in the south, Sharada in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Siddham, which carried Buddhist mantras to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Since the 19th century, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a> has become the usual script, and the order of its letters follows the classification of sounds by the ancient phoneticians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i.jpg" alt="Rudradaman I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudradaman_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploadalt, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In historical times Sanskrit was not the everyday language of the population, who spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrits</a> and their descendants, but the language of priests, scholars, poets and courts. In Sanskrit drama, kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> speak Sanskrit, while women and most other characters speak Prakrits, reflecting this division.</p><p>From the early centuries CE Sanskrit became the language of royal inscriptions and political culture over a vast area. The inscription of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> Rudradaman at Junagadh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, around 150 CE, is the first long royal inscription in the language, and under the Gupta empire, from the 4th century, Sanskrit literature and learning reached a classical peak. Rulers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> used Sanskrit in their inscriptions and took Sanskrit names: the oldest inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, from Kutai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> around 400, and many of the inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> are in Sanskrit. Historians have called this world a “Sanskrit cosmopolis”.</p><p>Sanskrit was also a language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. While the early Buddhist canon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> was preserved in Pali, a Prakrit, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> scriptures and philosophical works were written in Sanskrit, and their translation into Chinese and Tibetan carried Indian thought across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The great Buddhist monastic university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> taught in Sanskrit.</p><p>From the end of the first millennium the regional languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> developed literatures of their own, and Persian became the language of government under the Muslim dynasties, but Sanskrit remained the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ritual, law, philosophy and traditional learning. In modern times it is taught in schools and universities, broadcast on radio and printed in newspapers, and in a few villages, such as Mattur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, families have adopted it for daily use; in India’s 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> about 25,000 people named it as their mother tongue.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="Mahabharata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanskrit literature is one of the largest in the world. Its foundation is the Vedic corpus: the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, Rig, Sama, Yajur and Atharva, with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, Aranyakas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, the philosophical texts that profoundly shaped Indian thought.</p><p>The two great epics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, took shape over many centuries, roughly between 400 BCE and 400 CE. The Mahabharata, with nearly 100,000 verses in its longer versions one of the longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family and contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, the dialogue between the warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>; the Ramayana, attributed to the sage Valmiki, tells the story of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>. Both have been retold in every language of South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>Classical Sanskrit literature includes the poetry and drama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>, perhaps of the 4th or 5th century, whose play Shakuntala enchanted European readers when it was translated in 1789; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, encyclopedic collections of myth and history; the treatises on law and statecraft, such as the Laws of Manu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>; the Kamasutra; the theory of drama and aesthetic emotion of the Natyashastra; and the animal fables of the Panchatantra, which travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> and Arabic, as Kalila and Dimna, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Sanskrit was also the language of Indian science and philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a>, in 499, and Brahmagupta, in 628, wrote works of astronomy and mathematics in Sanskrit verse, Brahmagupta setting out rules for calculating with zero and negative numbers; the medical treatises of Charaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sushruta">Sushruta</a> founded Ayurveda; and the schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain philosophy, from Nagarjuna to Shankara, debated in Sanskrit for more than a thousand years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Vedic Sanskrit gradually developed into the classical language described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, after which Sanskrit was preserved by its grammar from further change. The spoken languages meanwhile evolved freely. The Middle Indo-Aryan languages, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrits</a>, included Pali, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist canon, and the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>’s edicts and of the Jain scriptures. These developed in turn into the modern Indo-Aryan languages, among them Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Bengali, Punjabi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Gujarati, Nepali, Odia and Sinhala, spoken today by more than a billion people.</p><p>Sanskrit enriched the vocabulary of all these languages, which have borrowed thousands of its words unchanged, and of the Dravidian languages of the south, such as Telugu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and Malayalam. Through trade, religion and courtly culture it reached the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: Thai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, Javanese and Malay contain many Sanskrit words, and the Indonesian word for language, bahasa, comes from Sanskrit bhāṣā.</p><p>English has borrowed words such as karma, yoga, guru, mantra, avatar and nirvana, and, through Hindi, jungle.</p><p>European scholars began to study Sanskrit seriously in the late 18th century. William Jones’s observation in 1786 that Sanskrit, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> shared a common origin, and the comparative grammar of Franz Bopp in 1816, founded the study of the Indo-European family, and Panini’s grammar influenced the development of modern linguistics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalidasa.jpg" alt="Kalidasa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalidasa_inditing_the_cloud_Messenger,_A.D._375.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Douglas Almond, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanskrit occupies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> a place comparable to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> together: it was for two thousand years the language of religion, learning and high culture across a vast region, and its literature, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and the epics to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> and the philosophers, is one of the great heritages of humanity.</p><p>It remains a living sacred language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> rituals, from daily worship to weddings and funerals, are performed in Sanskrit, and its mantras are recited by millions; Buddhist traditions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> preserve Sanskrit mantras and syllables, and the Jain tradition uses it in its scholarship.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s constitution lists Sanskrit among the languages of the nation, and the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> have made it a second official language. It is studied in schools and universities in India and around the world, and the tradition of Vedic recitation continues.</p><p>The modern discovery of Sanskrit transformed the study of language and history, revealing the kinship of peoples from Ireland to India, and the precision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>’s grammar remains a model for linguists and computer scientists.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sanskrit, the Indo-Aryan sacred and learned language of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, first known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> of around 1500–1200 BCE and codified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, was for two thousand years the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain scripture, the epics, philosophy, science and poetry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Its discovery by European scholars revealed the Indo-European family, and it remains the liturgical language of Hinduism.</em></p>

